  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Bauxite is the main ore of aluminium. It is a rock made from a mixture
    of minerals, rather than a single mineral. Minerals Education Coalition

    Formation:
    Bauxite commonly forms in warm, rainy climates through prolonged weathering of
    aluminium-bearing rocks. Rainwater washes away more soluble materials, leaving
    aluminium-rich minerals concentrated in the ground. Geoscience Australia

    Composition:
    Its main aluminium-bearing minerals are gibbsite, boehmite, and diaspore. It
    also contains iron compounds, silica, clay minerals, and other impurities. Their
    proportions vary between deposits. USGS

    Appearance: Bauxite usually has
    a dull, earthy appearance. Its colour can range from reddish-brown and yellow
    to tan or white. Minerals Education Coalition

    Uses: Bauxite is refined into
    alumina, which is then processed to produce aluminium metal. Aluminium is used
    in aircraft, vehicles, electrical cables, drink cans, and kitchen utensils.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
